WEBVTT

00:00:06.719 --> 00:00:10.199
A quantity form needs to explain different failures.

00:00:10.199 --> 00:00:13.412
A custom exception class names a failure for our

00:00:13.412 --> 00:00:14.215
application.

00:00:14.215 --> 00:00:17.561
InvalidQuantityException is checked: Java requires

00:00:17.561 --> 00:00:21.176
callers to catch it or declare that it may leave their

00:00:21.176 --> 00:00:21.644
method.

00:00:54.323 --> 00:00:57.395
The throws declaration names a possible failure.

00:00:57.395 --> 00:01:00.530
A throw statement raises an exception object now.

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:03.858
Integer dot parseInt converts decimal digits with an

00:01:03.858 --> 00:01:05.841
optional leading plus or minus.

00:01:05.841 --> 00:01:08.849
It rejects whitespace, invalid text, and values

00:01:08.849 --> 00:01:11.985
outside the int range with NumberFormatException.

00:01:11.985 --> 00:01:15.312
That type is unchecked, so callers need not catch or

00:01:15.312 --> 00:01:16.016
declare it.

00:01:16.016 --> 00:01:19.152
Our new exception keeps a message and an optional

00:01:19.152 --> 00:01:22.287
cause: an earlier failure that explains this one.

00:01:22.287 --> 00:01:25.743
Throwable is the parent type used here to receive that

00:01:25.743 --> 00:01:27.342
earlier exception object.

00:01:27.342 --> 00:01:30.350
Passing the cause to super preserves it for the

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:30.798
caller.

00:01:31.096 --> 00:01:34.316
The caller catches our checked exception separately

00:01:34.316 --> 00:01:35.262
for each input.

00:01:35.262 --> 00:01:38.545
It prints either the quantity or the problem message

00:01:38.545 --> 00:01:40.250
and whether a cause exists.

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:43.595
Trace the three inputs and predict every output line.

00:01:43.595 --> 00:01:44.921
Pause before running.

00:01:49.842 --> 00:01:51.943
Three becomes a valid quantity.

00:01:51.943 --> 00:01:55.400
The word two fails conversion, so the new exception

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:58.315
retains the original NumberFormatException.

00:01:58.315 --> 00:01:59.535
Has cause is true.

00:01:59.535 --> 00:02:02.924
Negative one converts successfully, then fails our

00:02:02.924 --> 00:02:04.077
nonnegative rule.

00:02:04.077 --> 00:02:07.534
There is no earlier exception, so its cause is null

00:02:07.534 --> 00:02:09.093
and Has cause is false.

00:02:09.093 --> 00:02:12.414
The catch block reports each failure and the loop

00:02:12.414 --> 00:02:13.092
continues.

00:02:13.148 --> 00:02:16.390
Change the final input from negative one to zero.

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:19.897
Predict the complete output and explain how the final

00:02:19.897 --> 00:02:21.948
call differs from the original.
